{
  "dom_id": "step-q113655",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n13≡\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{A}}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\nI. $3$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $9$",
  "worked_solution": "To visualize congruence modulo $5,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $5$ numbers. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14261972/assets/ec2bea08914f46aa5cc4cf96db3a65cf0d6eb17476dd715ebd97ce4d813879dd.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(13-3).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(13-3)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $5∤(13-5).$ Notice that $13-5=8,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $5∤(13-9).$ Notice that $13-9=4,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
